course: dictionary
lesson: 13124
title: Fluently
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fluently is an adverb. It tells you how someone does something. When you read or speak easily and smoothly, without stopping to search for words, you do it fluently. «Maya reads fluently, so she never stops to sound out the words.» The word fluently tells you her reading is smooth and easy.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «After one year in Spain, Tom speaks Spanish fluently.» A year is a long time to practise, and speaking without hunting for words shows skill. So fluently means easily and well, not just a little.
explain: Fluently can also describe movement. «The skater moved fluently across the ice.» Here the skater was smooth, with no jerks or stumbles. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «moved easily and smoothly across the ice» still make sense? It does, so you have it right.
fact: Fluently is an adverb: it tells how someone does something.
fact: Fluently means speaking, reading or writing a language easily, smoothly and well.
fact: «Fluently» can also mean moving or working smoothly, without stopping or stumbling.
fact: The adjective is fluent, as in «She is a fluent reader.»
fact: Useful phrases: «speak fluently», «read fluently», «fluently bilingual», which means knowing two languages well.
exercise: «Maya reads fluently, so she never stops to sound out the words.» What does fluently mean here? | Loudly and crossly | Quietly, in a whisper | *Easily and smoothly | Slowly, with lots of stops
exercise: «After one year in Spain, Tom speaks Spanish fluently.» What does this tell you about Tom's Spanish? | *Tom speaks it easily and well | Tom does not understand Spanish at all | Tom knows just one Spanish word | Tom is only learning to write his name
exercise: «The skater moved fluently across the ice.» Find and copy the word that tells how the skater moved. | moved | *fluently | skater | ice
test: Nadia says «read fluently» means reading very slowly. Is she right? | Only when reading in the morning | Only when reading out loud | *No, it means reading easily and smoothly | Yes, slowly is exactly what it means
test: «The old machine ran fluently all day without stopping.» What does fluently suggest? | *Working smoothly, without stumbling | Breaking down every hour | Being very tired and slow | Making a loud bang every time it starts
exam: «Ella is fluently bilingual: she speaks English and Welsh fluently.» What does fluently bilingual mean? | Only reads, and never speaks | Knows one language, but badly | *Knows two languages well and easily | Cannot speak any language at all
